  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MoCOMo &#x2014; Mobile COVID-19 monitoring in sewage for strong cities and regions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Information on the current status of the COVID-19 pandemic is vitally important for societies worldwide, especially in developing countries. Actionable, up-to-date and reliable data can be easily and cost-effectively gathered by quantitative monitoring of the coronavirus presence in sewage. The proposed technology for monitoring is mobile, easily deployable and provides results within two hours. The project aims at developing the mobile device and testing it in Nakuru and Kisumu. The implementing organisations are Orvion (the Netherlands) and Upande (Kenya), in association with local partners, including the utilities Nawassco and Kiwasco as well as Kenya Medical Research Institute. The main contributors are UN-Habitat and Aqua for All.At the start of the project, there were no field devices that could rapidly detect and quantify virus concentratiuons in water. The applicability of qPCR tests for COVID surveillance in waste water was already proven (e.g. by KWR in the Netherlands).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="4">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Innovate - Sanitation</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The pilot is focussed on Low Income Areas of Nakuru and Kisumu. The purpose of the device is to develop surveillance systems for Low Income Areas, globally.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project aims to:1. develop and pilot a field device for COVID-19 detection in waste water. The device is based on qPCR technology.2. 2. develop and test a data registration system and dashboard for almost real time COVID vigilance3. build up a business line for rapid COVID detection in low income areasThe pilot will have three components:1. development of the technology and device and data system2. piloting in Nakuru and Kisumu3. communication and building the business line</narrative>
  </description>
